add('### Regression specifications and estimates')
add('For continuous outcomes, OLS uses HC3 heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors. Binary TVK victory uses logistic regression with HC1 robust errors. Observations are constituencies, equally weighted. Outcomes are TVK vote share, TVK victory, DMK-alliance share change and turnout change. Rates and vote shares are measured in percentage points. Each model includes an intercept.\n\nThe primary **strict full** controls are2021 DMK+ and AIADMK+ shares,2021 margin percentage, categorical2021 winning party, log pre-SIR electorate,2021 turnout, electorate growth since2021, the panchayat-based urban proxy,2011 parent-district urban share and approximate log electorate density. **Strict district FE** replaces parent-district urban share with current district indicators, retaining the other controls. Current female composition, turnout change and net rebound are deliberately excluded from primary specifications because revision could affect them; additional sensitivity models include them. Prior turnout and growth are imperfect proxies and do not establish exchangeability.\n\nModel form: `Y_i = intercept + beta × DraftExclusionPct_i + controls_i + district effects + error_i`. For victory, this is the log-odds equation. Separate models substitute April net-reduction percentage. Coefficients, all nuisance terms, robust SEs, confidence intervals, p-values and model fit are in [strict models](tables/strict_models.csv), [strict coefficients](tables/strict_coefficients.csv), [additional models](tables/model_fit.csv) and [additional full coefficients](tables/regression_coefficients.csv).')
rr=[]
for outcome in ['tvk_share_2026','tvk_win','dmk_alliance_share_change','turnout_change']:
 r=m[m.model.eq(outcome+'__draft_exclusion_pct__unadjusted')].iloc[0];rr.append(dict(outcome=outcome,spec='Unadjusted',n=r.n,beta=r.coefficient,se=r.se,low=r.ci_low,high=r.ci_high,p=r.p,fit=r.pseudo_r2 if outcome=='tvk_win' else r.r2))
 for spec in ['strict_full','strict_district_fe']:
  r=s[(s.outcome==outcome)&(s.exposure=='draft_exclusion_pct')&(s.spec==spec)].iloc[0];rr.append(dict(outcome=outcome,spec=spec,n=r.n,beta=r.coef,se=r.se,low=r.ci_low,high=r.ci_high,p=r.p,fit=r.pseudo_r2 if outcome=='tvk_win' else r.r2))
add(tab(pd.DataFrame(rr)))
add('Fit is R² for OLS and McFadden pseudo-R² for logit; these are not directly comparable. Logit coefficients are log odds, not vote-share or probability changes. All fitted logits converged. Fixed-effects logits use193 seats in districts with both TVK wins and losses;41 seats in all-win/all-loss districts are excluded to avoid district-level separation. With small district groups and many controls, finite-sample bias remains a concern. A district-FE linear probability sensitivity retains234 seats and gives β=−0.00227, p=.843 (additional model includes current female share).')
add('**Substantive interpretation:** an unadjusted10-point increase in draft exclusion rate corresponds to5.68 points higher TVK share (95% CI4.56–6.80). Under strict full controls it corresponds to0.95 points (−0.77 to2.67); strict district FE gives−1.61 points (−4.13 to0.90). These are association contrasts, not predicted votes restored. The statewide strict full logistic coefficient is0.1119 (p=.0093): a10-point rate increase has an odds ratio3.06, with average local marginal effect about1.88 probability points per one rate point. District FE reverses its sign, β=−0.0481 (p=.503; OR per10 points0.62). Nonlinear marginal effects cannot be multiplied indefinitely or interpreted causally.\n\nDMK-alliance share decline is negatively associated with exclusions in some pooled adjusted models, but not district FE (β=+0.0207,p=.870). The fixed-label core-alliance sensitivity is also inconclusive within district (β=−0.163,p=.465). The latter is not a perfect constant-coalition vote:2026 DMDK seats then have no core-alliance candidate. No specification provides a robust estimate of selectively removed DMK support.')
add(img('12_adjustment_coefficients','Main vote-share estimates, including a check that excludes third-party map-derived density.'))
add('**Inference limits:** these234 seats are the entire election, not a random sample. Confidence intervals quantify model uncertainty under assumptions, not uncertainty about the observed descriptive totals. HC3 addresses unequal residual variance, not hidden confounding or all spatial dependence. District-clustered errors in the additional model give p=.181 for the draft-rate coefficient; a within-district Freedman–Lane residual-permutation check gives p=.235 (1,999 permutations). The latter additionally assumes within-district residual exchangeability. Many specifications are exploratory; nominal p≈.05 findings deserve caution. A nonsignificant coefficient is not proof of zero effect, especially with correlated controls and measurement error.')
add('## Confounding Factors')
add('### Urbanisation and migration')
add(tab(read('urban_groups'),['group','n','tvk_win_rate_pct','mean_tvk_share','mean_draft_exclusion_pct','mean_net_reduction_pct']))
add('The official panchayat crosswalk identifies31 seats with no listed village panchayats. They average28.64% draft exclusions and44.48% TVK share; the203 rural-containing/mixed seats average12.59% and33.24%. “Rural/mixed” includes suburbs and must not be read as a clean rural sample. The crosswalk’s reference vintage is unspecified and municipal conversions may be outdated. Census2011 district shares are old and contextual, and newly split districts inherit their parent share. No defensible contemporary AC-level migration rate was obtained.\n\nLog electorate density correlates0.793 with draft exclusion rate and0.655 with TVK share. District urban share correlates0.689 and0.493 respectively. These patterns fit the common-cause explanation: urban geography is associated with both. The coefficient shrinks82.2% when only urban controls are added, and83.3% under strict full controls relative to the unadjusted slope. These reductions are **descriptive attenuation**, not the percentage of a causal effect “explained away.” The non-FE model using only official urban proxies, omitting map-derived density, still has β=+0.187,p=.048; the comparable official-proxy district-FE model has β=−0.042,p=.757.\n\nThe migration component cannot be confirmed. Pre-SIR electorate growth since2021 has only weak correlation with draft exclusions (r=−0.118,p=.070) and TVK share (r=−0.079,p=.231). It combines migration, age eligibility, roll management and denominator differences. Administrative “shifted/absent” counts are part of the exposure, not an independent measure of legitimate migration. Within similar urban types, the relationship weakens: among31 urban-proxy seats the unadjusted slope is−0.029,p=.786; in Chennai’s16 seats it is−0.581,p=.0027. District comparisons also remove the positive slope. Yet rural/mixed and non-Chennai pooled samples retain positive adjusted associations under simpler controls. The evidence supports substantial geographic confounding, without validating every removal.')
add('### Previous political control')
add(tab(read('previous_party_deletions')))
add('DMK-held seats have a mean draft-exclusion rate15.90% versus13.23% for ADMK-held seats, a2.66-point difference. Welch’s t-test gives p=.0087, but Mann–Whitney p=.212 and the five-party Kruskal–Wallis test p=.190. Their medians,12.89% and12.28%, are much closer. This is consistent with differences concentrated in the upper tail, rather than a uniform shift. Net-rate Welch p=.029 also contrasts with Mann–Whitney p=.385. After urban geography, density and electorate size controls, the DMK-versus-ADMK coefficient is+0.406 points (95% CI−0.802 to1.615;p=.510). This does not establish targeted deletion by prior-party control. BJP has only four seats in this comparison. BusinessLine’s approximate mean net counts also check out:31,454 for DMK and26,953 for ADMK.')
