add('The net rebound after the draft is2,966,535 electors through April, so treating the9,737,831 draft exclusions as the final electorate loss overstates net reduction by that amount. This rebound may include new eligible voters, transfers and restorations, less subsequent removals; it is not an identified restored-voter count. A February news report cites approximately27.53 lakh additions and4.23 lakh later deletions during claims, consistent in scale with the official draft-to-February net increase2,330,624, but those rounded statewide gross flows cannot be assigned to ACs. [Deccan Herald](https://www.deccanherald.com/india/tamil-nadu/sir-in-tamil-nadu-final-voters-list-out-in-poll-bound-state-74-lakh-names-deleted-3909035).')
add(tab(cor[(cor.y=='tvk_share_2026')&(cor.method=='Pearson')],['x','r','ci_low','ci_high','p']))
add('Net rebound rate has the strongest unadjusted correlation with TVK share (r=.652), stronger than draft rate(.570) or net-reduction rate(.508). This undermines any simple story that a larger subtraction mechanically creates TVK support. In district-FE comparisons, none of the three rate coefficients is significant: draft−0.150,p=.244; net−0.184,p=.176; rebound+0.345,p=.532 (current-sex-composition sensitivity). Since draft rate = net-reduction rate + rebound rate, including all three would create exact collinearity. The supplied joint model uses only draft and rebound. Gross additions remain unknown; no conclusion about their causal effect is possible.\n\nStatewide ECI turnout increases from73.63% to86.03%, while ballots cast increase only6.57%, from46,344,550 to49,389,958. Holding the denominator at the reconstructed pre-SIR general electorate gives77.03% for2026. The approximately8.99-point difference from official2026 turnout is largely denominator arithmetic, with the service-elector coverage caveat. It is not an estimate of how SIR changed anyone’s propensity to vote. Draft rate remains strongly related to turnout change under FE (β≈.570,p<10⁻³⁸), as expected when deletion enters the denominator. Using turnout change as an explanatory control may block a mediator or induce mechanical bias; the negative post-revision-adjusted TVK coefficient is therefore not a primary causal result.')
add(img('08_turnout_change','Turnout change is strongly related to roll reduction, partly by construction.'))
add(img('09_turnout_denominator','Ballot-count growth and turnout-percentage growth are different quantities.'))
add('## Outcome-Sensitive Constituencies')
add('The draft-exclusion/margin ratio exceeds1 in211 seats,2 in167,5 in82 and10 in43. Such thresholds count entries, including legitimate omissions and later-restored people; they are not a test of election validity. All flags are in the master.\n\nDefine the **flip burden** B=(winning margin+1)/draft exclusions. It measures the net extra runner-up votes required per draft-excluded entry for an outright lead, rather than a tie. Define **Potentially outcome-sensitive constituencies** as B≤0.10. This screening threshold is defensible as an explicit feasibility scenario: half the draft-excluded entries are distinct eligible people still missing, half turn out, and their votes split70:30 towards the runner-up, yielding a net10% of D. It is a transparent triage rule, not a fitted probability, validated plausibility threshold or causal classification.\n\nForty-three seats pass:14 TVK,18 DMK+,11 AIADMK+. Because10 of these require B≤.025, they are especially suitable for individual-roll and complaint audits. A seat can be extremely close without being a high-deletion seat. Conversely, TVK’s large winning margins in many high-deletion seats make those seats less sensitive than the headline bands imply.')
add(tab(read('sensitivity_counts')))
add(img('07_deletion_margin_top30','The30 smallest flip burdens; logarithmic ratios prevent the one-vote seat from hiding the others.'))
add('### Top30: ranked audit priorities')
