add(tab(tt,rename={'net_rebound_draft_apr':'Net rebound (NOT gross additions)','minimum_q_at_t70_p60':'Required eligible/still-excluded q%, t70%, split60:40'}))
add('The [full top30 table](tables/top30_outcome_sensitive.csv) includes winner and runner-up names, previous winner name, net reduction, four turnout scenarios and blank gross-addition fields. Candidate names and numbers are retained in the master. The final column states what would have to be true: at70% turnout and60:40 runner-up preference, at least that percentage of draft exclusions must represent distinct eligible people still excluded and restorable. It is a continuous approximation; the exact integer grid is supplied. “Control changed” compares registered parties in2021 and2026.\n\nThe first-ranked seat is **AC185 Tiruppattur, Sivaganga**, with a one-vote margin; it is distinct from AC50 Tiruppattur. A one-vote reported result is confirmed in ECI’s detailed statistical report and contemporaneous reporting. A small number of genuine wrongful exclusions could matter there, but the margin alone says nothing about whether any occurred or favoured a party. [ECI detailed results](https://www.eci.gov.in/eci-backend/public/all_files/election_report/General_Election_to_the_Legislative_Assembly_of_Tamil_Nadu_2026_2026/10-Detailed_Results_1778165153.pdf); [Indian Express](https://indianexpress.com/article/india/tamil-nadu-results-2026-vijay-tvk-victory-stalin-dmk-loss-tiruppattur-one-vote-margin-10673427/).')
add('## Counterfactual Simulations')
add('Let D be draft-excluded entries; q the fraction representing distinct eligible people still missing at the election and restorable; t their turnout; pᵣ their runner-up preference and p𝓌 their winner preference. The runner-up gains the lead if **qDt(pᵣ−p𝓌)>M**. This is an accounting identity conditional on assumptions, not a causal model. Eligibility alone is insufficient: someone restored before polling or registered elsewhere cannot be added again.\n\nIn the two-candidate allocation p𝓌=1−pᵣ, so the continuous minimum preference is **pᵣ≥½[1+(M+1)/(qDt)]**. For the exact grid, N=floor(qDt) and the minimum runner-up votes are floor((N+M)/2)+1. Values aboveN or preferences above100% are infeasible. The two-candidate assumption sends every other restored vote to the winner; votes for third parties change the threshold. With a fixed fraction o voting for others, use pᵣ≥½[1−o+(M+1)/(qDt)], subject to0≤pᵣ≤1−o. There are no assumptions about preferences inferred from names or social identity.\n\nThe grid covers every seat, q=10%,25%,50%,100% and t=50%,60%,70%,75%. The deliberately extreme q=100% baseline is an upper-bound sensitivity: known deceased and duplicate categories make it inappropriate as an empirical estimate. The following top30 values show the minimum runner-up preference at q=100%; actual thresholds rise if q is smaller.')
cf=top[['ac_no','constituency','margin_2026']+[f'p_needed_q100_t{t}' for t in [50,60,70,75]]].copy()
for c in cf.columns[3:]:cf[c]*=100
add(tab(cf))
add('Scenario labels are tied to B, rather than subjective claims about how deleted people vote: **highly sensitive** B≤.025 (q=.25,t=.50,p=.60 suffices); **plausibly outcome-sensitive, scenario only** .025<B≤.10 (up to q=.50,t=.50,p=.70); **mathematically possible but demanding/implausible under those conservative scenarios** .10<B≤.75; **practically impossible within the requested turnout range** B>.75 (even q=1,t=.75,p=1 fails). The last class does not mean logically impossible at100% turnout or under a different deletion universe. The word “plausible” labels the assumed scenario only; we have no empirical distribution of q or p to estimate real-world likelihoods.')
add(tab(read('scenario_seat_counts').query('t==.7 and p==.6')))
add('These counts reverse whichever seat’s runner-up is considered; they do **not** apply one coherent statewide political preference and cannot be summed into a predicted alternative Assembly. At q=.50,t=.70,p=.60,30 margins are crossed:10 TVK wins and20 other wins. Whether restored voters actually preferred the runner-up is entirely unknown. See [exact counterfactual grid](tables/counterfactual_grid.csv) and [scenario counts](tables/scenario_seat_counts.csv).')
add('## Booth-Level Evidence')
add(f'''Official [2021 Form20 documents]({CEO}Form20_TNLA2021.aspx) are publicly linked for all234 ACs; the source inventory is included and AC001 was downloaded as a booth-level sample. The [official ASD portal](https://erolls.tn.gov.in/asd) exposes polling-part deletion lists through district/AC/part selection and CAPTCHA, and warns about old/new part-number correspondence. Thus it would be incorrect to say booth-level records do not exist. **A validated, machine-readable matched2021-booth/SIR panel was not assembled in this investigation. No booth-level coefficient is estimated.**

A credible extension would extract2021 candidate votes and electorate per polling area, obtain aggregated draft reasons and final restoration status, harmonise split/merged parts by geography, and then fit deletion rate on prior DMK/AIADMK shares with AC fixed effects. It must distinguish administrative labels from true eligibility, cluster uncertainty appropriately, and assess differential missing matches. Simply matching booth numbers across years is unsafe. Public elector names were not collected for this analysis, and no political preference is inferred from names, religion or caste.

Independent field reporting provides relevant contrary evidence to a claim that all deletions were legitimate. TNIE examined selected high-deletion booths and reported living residents classified as deceased or otherwise omitted; some had subsequently applied to re-enrol. It investigated a selected sample, not a probability sample of final exclusions. Its comparison of2024 turnout plus2025 omissions exceeding100% is not itself a logical impossibility: time, migration, deaths and roll changes can overlap those groups. The reported individual cases are more probative of draft errors than that arithmetic. They still do not establish final-election exclusion or party preference. [TNIE,18 January2026](https://www.newindianexpress.com/states/tamil-nadu/2026/Jan/18/sir-the-numbers-are-not-adding-up-in-tamil-nadu).''')
add('### Who was recorded as deleted?')
reason=pd.DataFrame({'Category':['Deceased','Shifted/absent','Multiple enrolment'],'Entries':[d.deceased.sum(),d.shifted_absent.sum(),d.duplicate.sum()]});reason['Share of exclusions %']=100*reason.Entries/d.draft_exclusions.sum();add(tab(reason))
add('Documentation-related and other categories cannot be separately counted from the published AC summary. “Shifted/absent” combines different administrative situations; it does not establish permanent migration. In the highest deletion-rate fifth, TVK-winning seats have83.12% of exclusions labelled shifted/absent,14.54% deceased and2.34% duplicate. The corresponding shifted/absent shares are77.41% in DMK+ wins and63.34% in AIADMK+ wins, based on just6 and3 seats respectively. In the lowest fifth, TVK-winning seats’ shifted/absent fraction is54.43%. This composition is consistent with an urban/mobile roll-cleaning mechanism, but also with errors in an ambiguous category. It cannot distinguish those explanations. [Reason-composition table](tables/reason_composition.csv).\n\nReason-specific FE models do not show a significant positive TVK relationship after the supplied controls: shifted/absent-rate β=−0.070,p=.603; deceased-rate β=−0.965,p=.055; duplicate-rate β=−2.086,p=.366. Categories are shares of pre-SIR electorate, not shares of exclusions; they are tested separately. These exploratory models are not evidence that any category “hurt” TVK. TNIE separately reported inconsistent absent/shifted labelling, reinforcing the measurement caveat. [TNIE,20 January2026](https://www.newindianexpress.com/amp/story/states/tamil-nadu/2026/Jan/20/absentshifted-arbitrarily-used-to-drop-voters).')
add('## Geographic Analysis')
add(img('10_geography_rates','All234 ACs; third-party2021 boundaries are illustrative, not an official boundary determination.'))
add(img('11_geography_outcomes','Election outcomes, turnout changes and scenario-screen flags; a sensitive flag does not indicate a proven changed result.'))
city=read('districts');add(tab(city[city.group.isin(['Chennai','Chengalpattu','Thiruvallur','Kancheepuram','Coimbatore','Madurai','Tiruchirappalli','Salem'])],['group','n','tvk_wins','mean_tvk_share','mean_draft_exclusion_pct','mean_turnout_change']))
add('District averages mix city and hinterland seats. Chennai’s16 ACs are not the entire metropolitan area; Chengalpattu, Kancheepuram and Thiruvallur capture surrounding areas only approximately. Coimbatore has high-deletion TVK losses such as Thondamuthur and Coimbatore South; Madurai, Tiruchirappalli and Salem also contain heterogeneous city/rural seats. No city-level inference is made from a whole-district mean.\n\nBoth variables cluster geographically: Moran’s I=.700 for draft rate and.443 for TVK share, each permutation p=.0005. Residual I is−.054,p=.143 after the additional district-FE model. The graph is a symmetrised six-nearest-neighbour graph, row-standardised, using approximate polygon-vertex centres;1,999 random permutations. Results are indicative and depend on the neighbourhood definition; this is not an official spatial model or proof that all spatial confounding has been removed.')
add('## Robustness Tests')
r=read('robustness');add(tab(r[r.exposure.eq('draft_exclusion_pct')],['subset','adjusted','n','coef','ci_low','ci_high','p']))
add('The subset “adjusted” models use a common parsimonious set:2021 DMK+/AIADMK+ shares, log electorate density and2021 turnout. They are not the same as the full or district-FE models. Chennai-only n=16 is particularly fragile with multiple controls. Raw-count and net-rate versions of every listed check are supplied in [robustness.csv](tables/robustness.csv). Removing the ten largest **net** reductions is separately tested in [exclude_net_outliers.csv](tables/exclude_net_outliers.csv).\n\nThe broad positive association survives raw versus rate measures, exclusion of Chennai, removal of large draft-deletion seats and exclusion of Edappadi. It is not uniformly robust: within urban-proxy seats it is absent; within Chennai it is negative; district FE removes the positive relationship; simple non-Chennai/rural-mixed adjusted models remain positive. Rate quintiles/deciles and alternative fixed percentage bands preserve a broad cross-sectional gradient but not strict monotonicity. Including2021 MNM share in the strict FE model leaves β≈−0.162,p=.214. The main result therefore cannot be reduced either to “Chennai alone explains it” or “controls prove the deletion effect.”')
add('## Evidence Supporting an SIR Effect')
add('The strongest supporting observations are the substantial raw association, the size-normalised gradient, positive statewide adjusted win models, documented draft-stage mistakes and some extremely close margins. Excluding Chennai does not eliminate the pooled association. These are reasons to investigate final exclusion and restoration records. They are not sufficient to attribute the association to wrongful disenfranchisement: legitimate roll cleaning, urban political change and turnout denominators can produce the same aggregate patterns.')
add('## Evidence Against an SIR Effect')
add('The strongest counter-evidence is the pronounced attenuation with geography, loss of a positive association within districts, absence of a positive within-urban gradient, opposite Chennai slope, higher correlation with post-draft rebound, and outcome-sensitive seats belonging to multiple alliances. Administrative reasons also include deaths and multiple enrolments, which cannot simply be treated as living opposition voters.\n\nConcrete counterexamples matter. Dharapuram had19.15% draft exclusions but TVK received only23.23% and finished behind both major alliances. Harbour had38.72% exclusions yet DMK won by11,750. Conversely, low-rate Ottapidaram (8.88%) gave TVK a29,083-vote margin; Krishnagiri (9.00%) an18,844 margin. These do not refute every possible local effect, but contradict a deterministic deletion-to-TVK mechanism. Full counterexample tables are included.')
add('### Political claims and outside analyses')
add(f'''BusinessLine reports competing party explanations rather than proof of a mechanism. Later public claims even conflict within the supposed beneficiary camp: TVK minister Rajmohan argued that SIR cost TVK a majority; VCK’s Ravikumar argued the DMK side suffered. AIADMK’s Palaniswami earlier welcomed removals as validating roll revision, while BJP’s Vanathi Srinivasan challenged opponents to identify eligible voters still removed. These are **political claims**, not measurements of eligibility, restoration or voting preferences. [TVK/VCK claims](https://www.newindianexpress.com/amp/story/states/tamil-nadu/2026/Sep/30/sans-sir-tvk-wouldve-won-more-seats-formed-majority-govt-minister-rajmohan); [AIADMK statement](https://www.newindianexpress.com/amp/story/states/tamil-nadu/2025/Dec/20/majority-of-deleted-entries-were-bogus-voters-aiadmk-chief-palaniswami); [BJP challenge](https://www.newindianexpress.com/amp/story/states/tamil-nadu/2026/Oct/05/bjp-challenges-tvk-congress-on-voter-deletions-after-sir-drive).

The search found secondary restatements of BusinessLine and independent field reports, but no identified peer-reviewed causal estimate of this2026 SIR–TVK question as of the cutoff. That is a search limitation, not a claim that no unpublished study exists. ECI’s official SIR process description and published categories explain the administrative exercise; they do not, by themselves, validate each exclusion. [ECI/PIB process statement](https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=2186480&lang=2&reg=48).''')
add('## What We Cannot Know From These Data')
add('The aggregate tables do not identify how many distinct eligible voters remained wrongly excluded at polling; how many draft omissions were restored; how many registered elsewhere; whether deletion reasons were accurate; or their turnout and political preferences. Gross final-period AC additions/deletions are missing. Contemporary AC urbanisation, true migration, age composition and other demographics are not adequately observed. The booth panel required for within-AC political-selection tests has not been constructed.\n\nAll constituencies experienced SIR. There is no untreated comparison group, random assignment, valid instrument or discontinuity that identifies a causal effect. Geography and previous vote controls reduce some confounding but cannot solve ecological inference: a DMK-held constituency is not a list of DMK voters. The analysis cannot identify individual political targeting or reconstruct an alternative Assembly. It also cannot rule out consequential wrongful exclusions in a handful of close seats. Large statewide associations and small local causal effects may coexist; the reverse is possible too.\n\nStatistical significance and political significance differ. The unadjusted p-values are tiny, yet the association may largely reflect geography. A district-FE estimate near zero does not make a one-vote contest safe from a small local error. Restored-voter effects are bounded only by assumptions about q,t and preferences; there is no credible estimated number of seats changed here.')
add('## Final Assessment')
add(tab(pd.DataFrame([
['Correlation','Yes—strong descriptive association','Draft rate r=.570; top/bottom fifth TVK share difference12.59 points; BusinessLine counts reproduced.'],
['Robustness','Partial and specification-dependent','Positive in several pooled models; attenuated by urban controls; absent/reversed with district FE; statewide logit remains positive.'],
['Electoral relevance','Real arithmetic vulnerability, actual impact unknown','43 seats meet an explicit scenario screen, including14 TVK wins; eligible still-excluded counts and preferences unknown.'],
['Causation','Not established','No final wrongful-exclusion measure, preference evidence, matched booth test or credible causal design.']
],columns=['Question','Answer','Evidence standard'])))
add('**“SIR deletions materially altered the2026 Tamil Nadu Assembly election outcome”: Suggestive but inconclusive.** There is specific reporting of draft errors and enough close constituencies for modest local errors to be consequential. But no chain connects those cases to final exclusion, actual missing votes and changed winners. The rating applies to the possibility of material local consequences; there is no demonstrated change in the statewide governing outcome.\n\n**“SIR deletions disproportionately benefited TVK”: Weak evidence.** The observed association is strong, but the causal-benefit proposition lacks robust within-geography support and voter-level mechanism evidence. Some pooled win models remain positive and prevent a categorical dismissal; they do not establish causation. The results also do not justify “evidence contradicts the claim,” because unmeasured local effects remain possible.\n\nThe most informative next evidence would be an audited final-roll restoration panel for the43 screened seats, followed by a geographically harmonised booth analysis and a defensible assessment of eligible voters still omitted. Until then, the defensible conclusion is **a verified aggregate association, substantial geographic confounding, and unresolved local electoral consequences**.')
add('## Reproducibility and delivery notes')
add('The accompanying archive contains the master, candidates, dictionaries, source registry, validation results, full model tables, scenario grid, all figures and the analysis code with cached source inputs. See [README](README.md). Fixed random seed:20261006. The report and plots use the same234-row master; all unavailable variables are preserved as missing. ECI statistical reports themselves state that statutory Returning Officer forms prevail in a dispute; this analysis follows the published statistical tables and does not adjudicate contested counts. Raw news articles are not redistributed. Official data snapshots and their hashes make the calculations reproducible even if live sites change. The2021 workbook has malformed style metadata, so a small XML reader extracts cell values without altering the original file. Source PDFs are preserved alongside extracted text.\n\nNo elector-level identities, inferred sensitive characteristics, political-preference labels or unsupported booth coefficients are included. The work is observational and exploratory; its model choices and scenario thresholds are disclosed rather than represented as preregistered.')
